|> Section
    handle=_bt3_start_

    *Right triangles.*
    A

    __right triangle__

    is a triangle in which one of the angles is 
    $90^\circ$. 
    These are right triangles:
    !! We use “” to mark a right angle:


    !! For example, this is a right triangle:
    !! the latter also being known as a

    !! __right angle__

    !! of that triangle. These are right triangles:

    |> Image
        src=tmp-images/ABC_right_triangle.svg

    !! And this is not:

    !! |> Image
    !!     src=tmp-images/ABC_not_right_triangle.svg

    !! (The “” mark is our way of indicating a
    !! right angle.) 
    !! ...so the above is a _right triangle_.
    !! ...so the above is an example of a 
    !! said ~_right triangle_~. (It has a right
    !! angle.)
    !! These become progressively flatter, leading 
    !! into the question of
    There is also the question of 
    whether a completely flattened triangle is
    a right triangle, or not:

    |> Image
        src=tmp-images/ABC_flattened.svg

    The answer is that this is

    _|NOT|_

    a right triangle 
    (nor the “point triangle” with $\A = \B = \C$, for
    that matter)
    because angles at $\A = \B$ are not 
    !! defined—you
    !! need two segments with length $>0$, to define
    !! an angle. In fact, as the right angle of a right triangle 
    !! requires two sides of length $>0$ on either side
    !! one can deduce that
    defined. In fact, as the right angle
    of a right triangle, or any other
    angle, needs two sides of length $>0$ 
    on either side to be well-defined, like so...
    |> Image
        src=tmp-images/ABC_flattened.svg
    ...one can deduce that

    __all three sides__

    of a right triangle have nonzero length,
    and that

    __all three angles__

    of a right triangle are (well-defined, and)
    nonzero, as well. This also implies that there is

    !! |> Image
    !!     src=tmp-images/ABC_healthy_right.svg

    !! This implies there is

    _|ONLY ONE|_

    right angle in any right triangle, 
    with the other two angles being
    $<90°$,
    or at least
    Euclideanly. 
    (If we depart from the Euclidean
    realm, e.g., do geometry on a sphere, it is
    easy to come up with triangles
    that have more than one $90^\circ$ angle,
    such as this one
    here:

    |> Image
        src=tmp-images/spherical_3_90.svg

    But non-Euclidean geometry is 
    off-topic at the moment.)
    !! not our topic here.)

    The second point to know about right triangles
    is that the “two other” angles 
    (that are not the right angle) sum to $90^\circ$,
    because all three angles must sum to $180^\circ$
    together. 
    
    !! For example, 
    !! if one of these angles is $70^\circ$,
    !! the other is $20^\circ = 90^\circ - 70^\circ$:
    !! the angles could be $90^\circ$, $70^\circ$
    !! and $20^\circ = 90^\circ - 70^\circ$:

    !! |> Image
    !!     src=tmp-images/spherical_3_90.svg

    !! Or $90^\circ$, $71^\circ$, and $19^\circ = 90^\circ - 71^\circ$:
    !! Or if it's $71^\circ$, the third is $19^\circ = 90^\circ - 71^\circ$:
    !! Or $71^\circ$ pairs with $19^\circ = 90^\circ - 71^\circ$:

    !! |> Image
    !!     src=tmp-images/spherical_3_90.svg

    !! (Etc.)

    |> Pause
    _Vocabulary #1._ The “non-right angles” of a right
    triangle are called the _acute angles_ of the
    triangle, because “acute” means “less than $90^\circ$”.
    (Which they are.)

    !! |> Pause
    !! _Vocabulary #1._ Two angles that sum to $90^\circ$
    !! are called _complementary_. The two non-right angles
    !! of a right triangle are therefore complementary!

    |> Pause
    _Vocabulary #2._ Two angles that sum to $90^\circ$
    are called _complementary_. The two acute angles of 
    a right triangle are therefore _complementary_!!

    !! |> Pause
    !! _Vocabulary #3._ Two angles that sum to $180^\circ$
    !! are called _supplementary_. 

    !! |> Section
    !! |> Pause

    !! (Nb: If you have to choose between
    !! remembering  

    !! __complementary__

    !! and

    !! __supplementary__

    !! then remember
    
    !! __complementary__
    
    !! and the associated
    !! fact about the non-right angles of a right triangle.)

    !! |> Example

    !!     If one acute angle of a right triangle is $70^\circ$,
    !!     then the other is $20^\circ = 90^\circ - 70^\circ$: 
    !!     !! by the complementarity:

    |> Example
        handle=_theta_triangle_

        !! If one non-right angle of a right triangle is $\theta$,
        !! the other non-right angle is $90^\circ - \theta$, by complementarity:
        If an acute angle (i.e., non-right angle) of a
        right triangle is $\theta$,
        the other one is $90^\circ - \theta$, by complementarity:

    |> Example

        If we drop a height from the right angle 
        of the triangle of Example >>_theta_triangle_
        to the opposite side, one more copy of 
        “$\theta$” and one more copy of “$90^\circ - \theta$”
        emerge in the drawing, by complementarity inside 
        the two sub-right-triangles:

        |> Pause

        (In particular, the two sub-triangles are
        similar to the original triangle, just at a
        smaller scale: but this, we already know from
        the fact that the height lands at $90^\circ$,
        at which point two equal angles—and therefore,
        the third—are locked into place.)

    !! |> StarDivider

|> Section

    *“Hypotenuse” and “legs”.*
    The side opposite the right angle of a
    right triangle is called the _hypotenuse_.
    It is the longest one. We refer to the
    two other sides as _legs_:


|> Section
    *The insight.* Label the two legs of a 
    right triangle as $a$ and $b$, the 
    hypotenuse as $c$:

    |> Pause

    Obviously, at the back of our minds, we
    sense that $c$ is uniquely determined (no
    choice!) once $a$ and $b$ are determined.
    But what is the formula? (Or is there any
    “nice” formula at all? Maybe there isn't.)

    The key insight is contained in this diagram:

    |> Pause

    The reason this diagram is a key insight is
    that $c^2$, the area of this square...

    |> Pause

    ...is determined by these two other areas,
    given here...

    |> Pause

    ...that are “$a$, $b$-stuff”: the area of the  
    full square on the left, call it $\A_1$, is $(a+b)^2$...

    |> Pause

    ...while the area of the four triangles on
    the right, that we may collectively call $\A_2$,
    is $4\cdot{1\over 2}ab$...

    |> Pause

    ...so altogether,

    $$
    c^2 = \A_1 - \A_2 = (a+b)^2 - 4\cdot{1\over 2}ab
    $$

    or 

    $$
    c = \sqrt{(a+b)^2 - 4\cdot{1\over 2}ab}
    $$

    by taking a square root! Though, in reality,

    $$
    - 4\cdot{1\over 2}ab = -2ab
    $$

    and

    $$
    (a+b)^2 = a^2 + b^2 + 2ab
    $$

    leading to the amazingly simple...

    |> Boxed
        $$
        c = \sqrt{a^2 + b^2}
        $$

    ...or..

    |> Boxed
        $$
        \,\,c^2 = a^2 + b^2\,\,\up{1.1}\dn{0.3}
        $$

    ...after squaring again!!! The latter equation
    is known as the _Pythagorean theorem_.

    |> Pause

    _Note on dimensions._
    Note that in

    $$
    \te{“}c^2 = a^2 + b^2\te{”}
    $$

    we equate a length squared with a sum of lengths
    squared—apples to apples, as should be—whereas in

    $$
    \te{“}c = \sqrt{a^2 + b^2}\te{”}
    $$

    we equate a length to a square root of sums of
    lengths squared, which is also “apples to apples”.
    (The square root of an apple squared is an apple.)

|> Section

    *The flat case.*
    We spent some energy above
    ranting against flat triangles being not right
    triangles. Nonetheless—and as some might say,
    “by continuity”—they respect the Pythagorean
    theorem:

    |> Pause

    (If the mark of a right triangle is that it
    respects the Pythagorean identity, then maybe
    flat triangles are right triangles after all.)
    (Actually, a triangle with $\A = \B$ is an
    !! flat triangle is an

    __orthogonal triangle__

    !! which is an inside joke you needn't worry about.)
    !! (It's hilarious, though, trust us.)
    which is a small inside joke of kinds.)

    |> Example

        The hypotenuse of a right triangle with two
        legs of length $1$ is $\sqrt{2}$:
        !! If the two legs of a right triangle
        !! are $1$, the third side 

    !! |> Example

    !!     The hypotenuse of a right triangle with two
    !!     legs of length $3$ is $3\sqrt{2}$:

    !! |> Example

    !!     The hypotenuse of a right triangle with two
    !!     legs of length $100$ is $100\sqrt{2}$:

    |> Example

        The hypotenuse of a right triangle with a
        leg of length $1$ and a leg of length $2$
        is $\sqrt{1^2 + 2^2} = \sqrt{5}$:

|> Section

    *Solving for a leg.*
    The fundamental equation 

    $$
    !! c^2 = a^2 + b^2
    a^2 + b^2 = c^2
    $$

    implies:

    |> Boxed
        $$
        a^2 = c^2 - b^2
        $$

    |> Boxed
        style=margin-top:0em
        $$
        b^2 = c^2 - a^2
        $$
    
    !! can be rearranged to solve for either $a$ or
    !! $b$, for example $a$:
    Or:

    |> Boxed
        $$
        a = \sqrt{c^2 - b^2}
        $$

    |> Boxed
        style=margin-top:0em
        $$
        b = \sqrt{c^2 - a^2}
        $$

    Or:

    |> Boxed
        $$
        \te{LEG1} = \sqrt{\te{HYPOTENUSE}^2 - \te{LEG2}^2}
        $$

    !! Or:

    |> Boxed
        style=margin-top:0em
        $$
        \te{LEG2} = \sqrt{\te{HYPOTENUSE}^2 - \te{LEG1}^2}
        $$

    !! It doesn't matter which is “leg1”, which is “leg2”.
    !! ...while rearranging.
    ...that are symmetric equations, it doesn't matter
    which is “leg1”, which is “leg2”.

    !! Thus, the Pythagorean theorem can also be used to 
    !! compute a leg, knowing the hypotenuse and the other
    !! leg.
    !! other two sides.
    !! one of the legs in terms of the hypotenuse 
    !! and the other leg.

    |> Example
        handle=_ladder_example_

        If a $6$m ladder is leaning against a wall with
        its base $1$m from the edge of the wall, the ladder
        reaches a height of

        $$
        \sqrt{(6\m)^2 - (1\m)^2} = \sqrt{36\m^2 - 1\m^2} = \sqrt{35\m^2} \approx 5.92\m
        $$

        by using the “$a = \sqrt{c^2-b^2}$” equation, with
        $b = 1\m$, $c = 6\m$:

        |> Pause

        _Note on units in Example >>_ladder_example_##._
        Please do pay attention to the fact that

        $$
        \sqrt{\m^2} = \m
        $$

        [“square root of meters squared equals meters”] in
        this example. The answer should be a number of meters,
        so this checks out.
        !! , so that “units work out”.

|> Section

    *Distance formula in the plane.*
    Take arbitrary points $P = (x_1,y_1)$, $Q = (x_2,y_2)$ in
    the plane:
    !! draw an orthogonal triangle with $P$ and $Q$ as corners:
    !! Imagine a right triangle whose corners are
    !! given by coordinates $(x_1, y_1)$, $(x_2, y_2)$,
    !! and, say, $(x_1, y_2)$:

    |> Pause

    Use 
    !! $(x_1, y_1)$, $(x_2, y_2)$ 
    $P$, $Q$ 
    as the two corners
    of an axis-aligned 
    |> del
        right
    orthogonal
    triangle:

    |> Pause

    Then, a few observations: 

    |> Pause

    1. $c = \overline{PQ}$, 
    were “$\overline{PQ}$” means the distance from
    $P$ to $Q$
    !! the hypotenuse of the triangle, is the 
    !! distance from $(x_1, y_1)$ to $(x_2, y_2)$; we 
    !! will “$\overline{PQ}$” for this distance, below

    |> Pause

    2. $a^2 = (x_2 - x_1)^2$, regardless of whether
    $x_1 < x_2$ or not (a minus sign in “$x_2 - x_1$” is washed away
    by the square)

    |> Pause

    3. $b^2 = (y_2 - y_1)^2$, regardless of whether
    $y_1 < y_2$ or not (a minus sign in “$y_2 - y_1$” is washed away
    by the square)

    |> Pause

    Thus

    $$
    c = \sqrt{a^2 + b^2}
    $$ 

    becomes 

    $$
    \overline{PQ} = \sqrt{(x_2 - x_1)^2 + (y_2 - y_1)^2}
    $$ 

    after taking these observations into
    account, or “the distance from $P$ to $Q$ is 
    $\sqrt{\textit{...}}$”. We refer to the  square 
    root

    |> Boxed

        $$
        \sqrt{(x_2 - x_1)^2 + (y_2 - y_1)^2}
        $$

    as the _distance formula_ 
    for the distance 
    from a point
    $(x_1, y_1)$ to a point $(x_2, y_2)$ in the
    plane. This is also

    __Pythagorean distance__

    or

    __Euclidean distance__

    depending on the speaker and place.
        
